@dataclasses.dataclass(frozen=True)
class ReadmeArgs:
    """Derive the public README's factual half from the running tool.

    Every count in that file was hand-typed and every one had drifted: 24 checks against a
    live 25, 54 fixtures against 55, a verb table frozen at nineteen while the CLI reached
    fifty-seven. `gb gatesdoc` now FAILS on exactly that drift, which is the right alarm and
    the wrong remedy — an alarm that fires on a number a human has to retype will keep firing.

    So the README has two halves and only one is written by a person. The hero, "What this
    is", "Install" and "What it does not do" are HANDWRITTEN: judgement, voice, and the
    promises a tool makes. The verbs, exit codes, gate, selftests and shipped trees are
    DERIVED, strictly between `<!-- gb:derived:begin -->` and `<!-- gb:derived:end -->`, and
    every byte outside those markers is preserved — a selftest leg proves it, because a
    generator that eats prose gets switched off within a week and then the counts rot again.

    `--check` exits 1 when the block is stale, which is what makes this gated rather than
    remembered. The build STAMP is deliberately not rendered: it hashes every producer on
    disk, so printing it would report the document stale on every commit until everyone
    learned to ignore the alarm. The doc carries the command; the command carries the value.
    """

    write: bool = arg(help="regenerate the derived block")
    check: bool = arg(help="fail when the derived block is stale, and write nothing")
    json: bool = arg(help="the measured facts as an envelope")
    selftest: bool = arg(help="prove this module's properties")


