class AdviseArgs:
    """Rank what to do next by joining every producer this repo already runs.

    Committed at 953bb31 and registered NOWHERE — absent from this command table AND from the
    exporter's allowlist, so it was a dead verb twice over: unreachable locally and unshipped
    publicly. Found by `gb dogfood audit`, which exists to find exactly this.
    """

    json: bool = arg(help="machine-readable envelope on stdout")


@dataclasses.dataclass(frozen=True)
class XArgs:
    """What practitioners are ACTUALLY doing with Grok Bot, ranked by distinct authors.

    `bin/gb-x-sweep.py` holds the ONLY use-case classifier in this repo and was reachable by
    no verb and shipped to nobody — the same double-dead defect as `advise`, found the same
    way. It produced 487 use-describing posts from 448 distinct authors (442 authored), and
    that number could not be re-derived through the CLI at all.

    Ranking is by DISTINCT AUTHORS, never by post count or engagement. Measured 2026-09-11:
    ranking by citations made one account talking to itself (`alltoolsverse`, 5x) look like a
    market, and an 866k-impression row I cited turned out to belong to a different @mattyp
    post about moving to San Francisco. `authored` excludes relays; in `agent-orchestration`
    that is the difference between 43 and 38, because all five relays are one SpaceXAI
    engineer. Cite the authored number.

    `reclassify` is OFFLINE — zero search calls, ~1s — so any figure here is reproducible
    without spending the rate limit, which is what makes it quotable.
    """

    action: str = arg(
        default="reclassify",
        help="reclassify (offline, from artifacts) | collect (live search) | matrix | fetch (gb-x.py firehose)",
        choices=["reclassify", "collect", "matrix", "fetch"],
        positional=True,
    )
    max_calls: Optional[int] = arg(
        help="hard ceiling on search calls; collect refuses to exceed it"
    )
    dry_run: bool = arg(help="show the query matrix without spending a call")
    apply: bool = arg(help="fetch: write the artifact (quota spent regardless)")
    json: bool = arg(help="machine-readable envelope on stdout")

@dataclasses.dataclass(frozen=True)
class PostArgs:
    """Posts that RUN their own numbers before they ship, and refuse themselves if they fail.

    Measured 2026-09-12, against a survey of 42 third-party Grok Bot sources: 11 of them
    (26%) carry a reproducible number at all. Every post this emits carries the command that
    re-derives it, because that 74% is the whole opening.

    The gate is not decorative. On its first run over eight real claims it refused two of
    them, both with the highest teaching value in the set — and both refusals were MY
    command's fault, not the claim's: a wrong dict key crashed the reproduce line. That
    exposed a second defect in the verifier itself, which was reporting a crashed instrument
    as a false claim. A traceback is now its own verdict and never impugns the claim.

    `draft` verifies and prints. `record` writes to the ledger and refuses to record anything
    that does not verify at that moment. `verify` re-runs everything already published, and a
    number that has MOVED is reported as drift rather than error — those are the best posts,
    because our own prior figure is the baseline nobody else has.
    """

    action: str = arg(
        default="draft",
        help="draft | verify | ledger | record",
        choices=["draft", "verify", "ledger", "record"],
        positional=True,
    )
    limit: Optional[int] = arg(help="stop after N publishable posts")
    out: bool = arg(help="also write posts/<ISO>.md")
    include_published: bool = arg(help="do not hide ledger repeats")
    ids: Optional[str] = arg(help="record: comma-separated claim ids")
    json: bool = arg(help="machine-readable envelope on stdout")


@dataclasses.dataclass(frozen=True)
class FindingsArgs:
    """The claim SUPPLY: every teachable thing the verbs can prove, bound to its command.

    Feeds `gb post`. The two are deliberately separate: this one asks "what is true and
    re-derivable", the other asks "is it worth telling anyone, and does the command still
    agree". Collapsing them would mean the thing that generates a claim also decides whether
    it passes, and a producer that grades itself is the failure mode this repo exists to avoid.

    `--verify-commands` re-runs every row's command and exits 1 on any that answers nothing.
    It caught two rotted commands the day it was written, both from jq precedence:
    `[...] | length, .plugins.total` parses as `[...] | (length, .plugins.total)`.
    """

    verify_commands: bool = arg(
        help="re-run every row's command; exit 1 if any fails to re-derive its row"
    )
    no_write: bool = arg(help="print only; do not write the artifact")
    json: bool = arg(help="machine-readable envelope on stdout")


@dataclasses.dataclass(frozen=True)
class TeachArgs:
    """What form a Grok Bot teaching post actually takes, measured over the collected corpus.

    The headline is the opening: 51 teaching posts in 3,026, from 34 distinct authors — 1.7%
    of everything said about this product explains HOW to do anything. Only 3 authors (9%)
    ever show a command, one author holds 100% of before/after framing, and thread-markers
    and video-chapters are ABSENT with a positive control proving the detector can see forms
    that are present.

    Precision is a CENSUS, not an estimate: all 77 detector hits were read by hand and each
    verdict recorded, giving 51/77 = 66.2%. Two CJK relay frames got past every rule and were
    found only by hand, one of which changed a verdict — so the rule set is known-incomplete
    and says so rather than implying coverage.
    """

    action: str = arg(
        default="forms",
        help="forms | absences | authors | posts | census",
        choices=["forms", "absences", "authors", "posts", "census"],
        positional=True,
    )
    write: bool = arg(help="write the teach/<ISO>.json artifact")
    json: bool = arg(help="machine-readable envelope on stdout")


@dataclasses.dataclass(frozen=True)
class RoadmapArgs:
    """The open product gaps, each re-proved on every run so the list cannot go stale.

    Four wizard duels produced 24 ideas and nothing could say which had shipped. An audit
    measured it: 2 shipped as named, 6 under different names, 8 partial, 8 never. Searching
    for the PROPOSED VERB NAMES — my own first method — found one and understated delivery
    by seven, because `gb delta` had shipped as `gb digest` and the routine witness as the
    gate check `g25-routine-liveness`.

    So every row here carries a command plus the signal that appears in that command's output
    WHILE the gap exists. A gap someone closes drops off by itself; nobody has to remember to
    delete a line. Rows may also carry a positive CONTROL the probe must see before a CLOSED
    verdict is believed — added after a real, unfixed gap reported CLOSED because its signal
    never appeared in its own command's output. A wrong signal fails in the flattering
    direction, which is the one nobody re-checks.

    Ranked by severity x reach, effort breaking ties only. The first ranking divided by
    effort and put a trivial README fix above a blocking gap every operator hits, which is
    exactly how a backlog fills with easy wins while the structural problem ages.
    """

    detail: bool = arg(help="full why/fix/proof for every open gap")
    json: bool = arg(help="machine-readable envelope on stdout")

